# Core Engineering Rules

## Before any multi-file change
List every file that will be touched and why before implementing. No surprises.

## Definition of done
- Builds without errors
- No obvious security regressions
- All error paths handled — no silent failures, no empty catch blocks
- Loading and error states present in any UI that fetches data

## Dependencies
- Do not introduce new packages or libraries unless explicitly requested
- If a new dependency is needed, name it and explain why before adding it

## Secrets
- Never commit secrets, API keys, or tokens to any file
- If a secret is spotted in code or history, stop and flag it immediately

## Defensive null handling
Every database call and external API call can return null, undefined, or an error. Handle it explicitly before using the result.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — crashes if org is null or brand_context is missing
const { data: org } = await getOrganization(supabase, orgId)
const voice = org.brand_context.voice

// ✅ GOOD — handled before use
const { data: org, error } = await getOrganization(supabase, orgId)
if (error || !org) {
  throw new AppError('Organization not found', 'NOT_FOUND', 404)
}
const voice = org.brand_context?.voice ?? ''
```

Never assume a nested property exists. Use optional chaining (`?.`) for access and nullish coalescing (`??`) for defaults. If a null value means the operation cannot continue, return an error early — don't let it propagate.

## Small, reviewable changes
- Prefer small focused changes over large rewrites
- If a change is large, propose a plan first and wait for approval
